---
import LearnLayout from "../../layouts/LearnLayout.astro";

const related = [
  {
    href: "/swiss-german-phrases/",
    kicker: "Phrasebook",
    title: "Swiss German phrases for daily life",
    description: "Use practical Zürichdeutsch for greetings, cafés, transport, and simple conversations.",
  },
  {
    href: "/zurich-german/",
    kicker: "Dialect",
    title: "What makes Zürichdeutsch distinct?",
    description: "Learn how the Zürich dialect relates to Swiss German and Standard German.",
  },
  {
    href: "/learn-swiss-german/",
    kicker: "Start here",
    title: "A practical route into Swiss German",
    description: "Choose one dialect and build useful recall from the German you already know.",
  },
];
---

<LearnLayout
  title="I hätt gärn: Meaning and Examples in Swiss German"
  description="Learn what “I hätt gärn” means in Swiss German. See practical Zürichdeutsch examples for cafés, shops, and polite requests."
  eyebrow="One phrase · Many useful moments"
  heading="I hätt gärn."
  intro="This Zürichdeutsch phrase means “I would like.” Use it to order, shop, and make a polite request without changing to Standard German."
  toc={[
    { id: "meaning", label: "Meaning" },
    { id: "examples", label: "Useful examples" },
    { id: "build", label: "Build the phrase" },
    { id: "spelling", label: "Spelling and dialect" },
  ]}
  related={related}
>
  <section id="meaning" class="learn-section">
    <span class="section-number">01</span>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Direct translation</p>
      <h2>Use it when you want something.</h2>
      <div class="translation-ticket">
        <div>
          <small lang="de">Standard German</small>
          <strong lang="de">Ich hätte gern …</strong>
        </div>
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        <div>
          <small lang="gsw">Zürichdeutsch</small>
          <strong lang="gsw">I hätt gärn …</strong>
        </div>
        <p>English: <b>I would like …</b></p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The phrase is polite and direct. Add the item after <i lang="gsw">gärn</i>, then finish with
        <i lang="gsw">bitte</i> when it feels natural.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="examples" class="learn-section">
    <span class="section-number">02</span>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">At the counter</p>
      <h2>Four requests you can use today.</h2>
      <div class="example-list">
        <article>
          <span>01 · Café</span>
          <h3 lang="gsw">I hätt gärn en Kafi.</h3>
          <p lang="de">Ich hätte gern einen Kaffee.</p>
          <small>I would like a coffee.</small>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02 · Bakery</span>
          <h3 lang="gsw">I hätt gärn es Gipfeli.</h3>
          <p lang="de">Ich hätte gern ein Croissant.</p>
          <small>I would like a croissant.</small>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03 · Restaurant</span>
          <h3 lang="gsw">I hätt gärn d Rächnig, bitte.</h3>
          <p lang="de">Ich hätte gern die Rechnung, bitte.</p>
          <small>I would like the bill, please.</small>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>04 · Shop</span>
          <h3 lang="gsw">I hätt gärn die da.</h3>
          <p lang="de">Ich hätte gern diese hier.</p>
          <small>I would like this one.</small>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="build" class="learn-section">
    <span class="section-number">03</span>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Make it productive</p>
      <h2>Keep the frame. Change the ending.</h2>
      <p>
        Learn the phrase as a reusable frame. Replace only the item until the beginning feels automatic.
      </p>
      <div class="phrase-builder" aria-label="Phrase building pattern">
        <span lang="gsw">I hätt gärn</span>
        <b>+</b>
        <div>
          <i lang="gsw">en Kafi</i>
          <i lang="gsw">es Gipfeli</i>
          <i lang="gsw">d Rächnig</i>
          <i lang="gsw">die da</i>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="learn-callout">
        <span>Practice route</span>
        <p>Read the German prompt. Say the Zürichdeutsch phrase before you reveal the answer.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="spelling" class="learn-section">
    <span class="section-number">04</span>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">No single standard spelling</p>
      <h2>You will see more than one written form.</h2>
      <p>
        Swiss German is mainly spoken. Writers choose spellings that fit their dialect, audience, and preferred sound detail.
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><b lang="gsw">I hätt gärn</b> is a readable Zürichdeutsch form used in this course.</li>
        <li><b lang="gsw">Ich hätt gärn</b> keeps more of the written Standard German form.</li>
        <li><b lang="gsw">gärn</b> and <b lang="gsw">gern</b> can both appear in informal writing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Do not stop speaking because another writer uses a different form. Listen for the shared phrase and stay consistent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</LearnLayout>
